Z||000000||Monkeyman||03-20-2000||09:57 PM||||I removed the inner sail panels in the Grape Ape today just to find that there weren't any cut outs for the speakers in the metal!  Damn!  Will I run into any problems if I cut into the metal or do I just need to sell my current speakers.  I can't quite afford the ones that I really want (which would eliminate the need for rears.).||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||03-20-2000||10:11 PM||||Hey Monkey! According to my Big Fiero Book, it says, "It will take some work and some metal cutting, but you can install quality 4" X 6" speakers into the later stock location." ||||reg||1
Z||000002||phillip||03-21-2000||01:46 AM||||i,m using 1 4" speaker in a pod that is part of my third brake light. works pretty well like a rear center channel(the other 4" is my front center speaker).||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||03-21-2000||02:02 AM||||Eventually, I plan on putting a set of 5 1/4" separates at my feet but those are around $225 (about what I paid for the 2 sets of stock [but upgraded] speakers I now have).  Maybe I should sell my current speakers (still in the boxes) and just get the separates.  WT III has 6 1/2" separates in his '87.  I haven't heard his system yet but I'll take his word for the sound quality.  (Supposed to be great!)  Now I just have to sell my old (new) speakers.||||reg||1
Z||000004||phillip||03-21-2000||10:47 AM||||i have 5 1/4" separates in my car (coustic,in an '87 ) i made sort of three sided pyrimid pods for them and they sit where the dead pedals are. the crossovers are where the front speakers were (good place for them, plus if you're still using the factory radio and wiring all you need is new wire to the speakers). <BR> i forgot to mention also that my rear 4" points at the back window(non directional rear fill is what i was going for). if you can find a housing and mod it you won't have to cut your panels or the metal behind them.need a small magnet speaker though.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||03-21-2000||05:09 PM||||Phillip,<P>I would be interested in hearing all about your system.  From what you have said, yours sounds to be the second best Fiero setup around.  Most people only know how to create more volume and not better quality sound.  Please fill me in on all your components, locations etc.  <P>My system is composed of all Polk speakers, Sony electronics (can get them cheap... not a fan of their stuff though) and an MTX amp.  I too have my mids in custom kickpanels resting on the dead pedal area.  My .75" tweeters are located in the bottom vents of the dash and my sub is temporarily located in the passenger side footwell.  I am working on getting an 8" sub to place in the dash.  I have an indash head unit and an indash 9-band EQ.  I have one amplifier (couldn't afford a separate for the sub) located behind the passenger seat.  Rounding out the system is my 6-disk changer.  I intend on mounting it to the roof fore of my 3rd brake light in Fierosound style.  My installation is far from complete, and won't be anytime soon, but right now it functions well enough to sound decent and shows promise of what the future holds.   <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000006||phillip||03-22-2000||01:51 AM|||| my system :jvc 3 disc in dash player reciever,profile 1/2 din eq gives me front and rear outputs and fading.the rear outs go into a 2 to 1 rca jack to give me a single rear out.next is aan old sound stream x-over with sub and center channel controls. it controls two amps i lucked out in finding . an sf90 runs all my smaller speakers (4 channels 2X30, 2X15).the two higher powered channels run my main left and right(5 1/4" seps) . one each of the lower powered channels run my front and rear center speakers <BR>(jensen ,i know not the best but they were  and are good enough for  now).i made a pod that is in the center of my dash for the front center. a d-60 (2X30 ,over 80 bridged) run a single jl audio 8w1 in a wood and fiberglass sealed box that is in the console- front foot well corner of the passenger side.<BR> if you're not into super high power and you can find these amps get them.they're tiny.i have them both in a soft computer case with the x-over sitting over them i cut two holes in the bag over them for fans(radio shack with pioneer ts-871 speaker grills that fit over them well).the bag sits over my spare tire with enough wire to move it around a little or with plugs on every thing take it out compleatly if i need to.<BR> in the future i'd like to work in some power windows so i can work an 8" sub into each door and use 3 12 ohm speakers. i also found a d 100 amp, also small, that needs repair but might be better with more subs.also there is a 1/2 farad cap in the bag. <BR>can you tell i like car stereo? since i did it my self and was patient looking for components the system was well under the $1000 dollar goal i set. even if i change subs and depending on the cost of the d-100 amp repair,i might still be close to my goal.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Loki||03-22-2000||03:14 AM||||This will be my first time on ths forum. As for all the stereo talk go here. <A HREF="http://www.cox-internet.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.cox-internet.com/fiero</A>     follow the stereo link. I belive I have it up to date. If you have any questions please feel free to ask.<P>Michael<P>||||reg||6
Z||000008||phillip||03-22-2000||09:50 AM||||just noticed something in your post thorn. it made me wonder if fiero sound is the same person that was in car stereo review a few years back. the over head cd changer idea made me wonder. i used that in my '74 vette.thats also what gave me the amp location idea,except he didnt have air or a sun roof.apperence wise mine would look better if i didnt have to work around those two options.||||reg||1
